From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pear <pear@localhost>
Date: Mon, 17 Sep 2001 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [storage] feat: the recurrent-state pages a later lookup no
 longer loads are demoted

Local, from no pull request.

For a hybrid (recurrent-state) model LMCache stores one recurrent-state page per chunk per object group, but a later
lookup on the same prefix loads only the last `window` chunks of such a group: the earlier pages are dead weight.
end_session touches every key of a session, so the dead pages stay as recent as live data and an eviction round
takes live prefixes instead. An on_l1_keys_demoted hook goes through the listener, L1Manager, StorageManager and
the eviction policy (the LRU moves demoted keys to its least-recently-used end and never promotes them again); the
pages outside the window are demoted where the prefetch controller already releases their read locks; end_session
touches only the usable keys (usable_key_indices), the window's pages first, so that the LRU's reversed walk leaves
them most recent. A key demoted by mistake causes an earlier miss, never wrong KV.
---
